<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Formosa</h1><p class="st-meta">1895 CE – 1895 CE · capital Taipei, Tainan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived republic proclaimed on Taiwan in 1895 to resist the island's cession to Japan, among the first republics declared in East Asia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> of April 1895 forced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> to cede <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-china">Taiwan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>. Local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> and officials, hoping for foreign intervention, proclaimed the Republic of Formosa on 25 May 1895 with the Qing governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-jingsong">Tang Jingsong</a> as president and a tiger flag.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was essentially a resistance government. It had a president, a parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> and its own stamps and banknotes, but it professed loyalty to the Qing and relied on Chinese garrison troops, local militias and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-flag-army">Black Flag Army</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yongfu">Liu Yongfu</a> in the south.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Japanese troops landed at the end of May; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-jingsong">Tang Jingsong</a> fled to the mainland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a> fell in early June. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yongfu">Liu Yongfu</a> continued resistance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tainan">Tainan</a> as leader, but the city fell in October 1895 and he escaped. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taiwan-under-japanese-rule">Taiwan under Japanese rule</a> followed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-formosa.jpg" alt="Republic of Formosa" width="320" height="291"><figcaption>Image: Henry Szytko, vectorised by cypp0847 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Formosa_(1895).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1895 CE – 1895 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taipei, Tainan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tang Jingsong, Liu Yongfu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hokkien, Hakka, Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Formosa Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1895&amp;focus=republic-of-formosa" aria-label="Republic of Formosa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7903 1256 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Formosa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8223 1512L7567 1068M8223 1512L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8223 1512h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8223 1512h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1895&amp;focus=republic-of-formosa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
